  "body": "  For a positive integer , a representation with integers exists if and only if is even at every prime . A representation with exists if and only if no such prime divides and .    In the norm of an Eisenstein integer, an inert prime has even exponent because conjugation fixes it. A split prime permits any exponent by using one conjugate factor, and powers of give every exponent at three. These choices prove both necessity and sufficiency for the first assertion.  For the primitive assertion, an inert prime dividing a norm forces the Eisenstein integer to vanish in , so it divides both coefficients. If nine divides the norm, reduction modulo three gives . Substitution gives ; hence three divides both coefficients. Conversely, use only split primes and at most one factor of . At each split prime choose the full required power of one conjugate prime, without the other. No rational prime then divides both coefficients. This gives the required primitive representation.   "
