  "id": "ck3-categorical-torelli",
  "level": "2",
  "url": "ck3-dictionary.html#ck3-categorical-torelli",
  "type": "Theorem",
  "number": "7.8",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  Let be smooth complex cubic fourfolds. They are isomorphic if and only if there is a complex-linear exact equivalence whose induced numerical isometry satisfies    A projective isomorphism induces an equivalence commuting with rotation, so gives the condition. Conversely, representability supplies an integral Mukai Hodge isometry extending . The commutation on preserves the sum of the two nontrivial rotation eigenspaces. Since is primitive and contained in , it is the intersection of that complex span with the integral Mukai lattice. Therefore sends the distinguished onto the distinguished for . Its restriction to their orthogonals gives an integral Hodge isometry . The classical Torelli theorem in implies . As explained there, an overall sign may be needed to make this primitive isometry extend to the full middle lattice fixing the hyperplane square. The existence of an isomorphism does not assert that the initially given primitive map is itself that pullback.   "
